WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.960
More wells cost an afternoon in the lab, not a field trip.

2
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.900
Pond S2 already gave twenty negative wells.

3
00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:14.200
Are those twenty tries, or only two?

4
00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:16.970
Draw S2 as a tree.

5
00:00:18.290 --> 00:00:20.250
The pond gives two blue water bottles.

6
00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:23.850
Each bottle gives two green extraction tubes.

7
00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:26.910
Each tube feeds five gold wells.

8
00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:30.990
So all twenty wells hang from just two scoops of water.

9
00:00:32.033 --> 00:00:33.653
Use the workshop's numbers.

10
00:00:35.013 --> 00:00:37.673
A scoop holds DNA 60% % of the time.

11
00:00:38.813 --> 00:00:40.473
That is theta, the blue line.

12
00:00:41.673 --> 00:00:46.593
The capture and well values are borrowed from a wild pig study in Texas, as

13
00:00:46.593 --> 00:00:47.713
illustrations only.

14
00:00:48.817 --> 00:00:53.857
Try adding wells. Pour more gold wells under the same two bottles.

15
00:00:55.097 --> 00:00:57.237
The gauge for one sample climbs at first.

16
00:00:58.517 --> 00:01:00.977
Then it levels off, still below the blue line.

17
00:01:02.217 --> 00:01:05.177
It never crosses theta, however many wells you add.

18
00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:07.580
The reason is simple.

19
00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:14.380
If the scoop held no DNA, every tube and every well from it is testing empty water.

20
00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:19.900
No number of wells can find DNA that the sample does not contain.

21
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.220
Watch the bar for the chance that S2 is still occupied.

22
00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:29.980
With unlimited wells on two samples, it stops at 7.2%.

23
00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:33.340
That is still above the 5% percent line.

24
00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:38.900
Now add a third blue bottle instead, a new scoop of water from the pond.

25
00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:45.020
With the usual five wells per extraction, the bar drops to 4.8%.

26
00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:47.380
That is below the line.

27
00:01:48.433 --> 00:01:49.993
Here is a tempting mistake.

28
00:01:51.573 --> 00:01:55.413
Suppose we pretend the twenty wells were twenty separate trips to the pond.

29
00:01:56.673 --> 00:01:59.973
Will the pond look emptier, or fuller, than the evidence allows?

30
00:02:06.567 --> 00:02:11.807
Emptier. Redraw the tree as twenty bottles, each with one well.

31
00:02:13.147 --> 00:02:14.867
Each one now looks like its own try.

32
00:02:16.067 --> 00:02:18.967
The bar plunges to about two percent, and you would stop.

33
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:23.360
But you would stop on an error in the model, not on evidence.

34
00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:29.160
Counting repeated measurements of one unit as if they were separate units has a

35
00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:32.280
name. It is called pseudoreplication.

36
00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:37.700
So wells and extractions from one sample all test the same water.

37
00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:42.720
They are second chances at the lab steps, not new tries at the scoop.

38
00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:46.200
Here, adding wells cannot cross the line.

39
00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:48.960
A third water sample does.
